%[text] # Lagrange Polynomial Interpolation
%[text] This script demonstrates how to construct a Lagrange polynomial that passes through a set of given points. Lagrange interpolation is a method for fitting a polynomial through data points without solving a linear system - instead, it directly composes basis polynomials.
%%
%[text] ## Lagrange Basis Functions
%[text] Each Lagrange basis function $ L\_j(x) $ is designed so that:
%[text] - $ L\_j(x\_j) = 1 $ (equals 1 at the j-th point)
%[text] - $ L\_j(x\_m) = 0 $ for all $ m \\neq j $ (equals 0 at all other points) \
%%
%[text] ## Step 1: Define the Function to Interpolate
%[text] We use a symbolic variable x for exact calculations and set interpolation parameters.

syms x
f(x) = sin(x)*exp(x);

N = 3;              % Number of interpolation points
Interval = [0 3];   % Domain of interest
%%
%[text] ## Step 2: Create the Interpolation Data
%[text] Generate equally-spaced sample points and evaluate the function at these points.

fplot(f,Interval,LineWidth=3) %[output:1bf85e32]
x_nodes = linspace(Interval(1),Interval(2),N);
y_nodes = vpa(f(x_nodes));

hold on %[output:1bf85e32]
for ii = 1:N %[output:group:44bdf6a1]
    scatter(x_nodes,y_nodes,30,"filled",SeriesIndex=2) %[output:1bf85e32]
end %[output:group:44bdf6a1]

%%
%[text] ## Step 3: Construct the Lagrange Polynomial
%[text] For each data point $ j $, we construct the basis polynomial $ L\_j(x) $ as a product of linear factors. The interpolating polynomial is then formed by summing the weighted basis functions.

Lagrange = 0;
for j = 1:N %[output:group:6b1b2e16]
    % Construct the j-th Lagrange basis polynomial
    % L_j(x) = product of (x - x_m)/(x_j - x_m) for all m ≠ j
    L_j = 1;
    for m = 1:N
        if j ~= m
            L_j = L_j*(x-x_nodes(m))/(x_nodes(j)-x_nodes(m));
        end
    end
    fplot(L_j,Interval,LineStyle="--") %[output:652f9e87]
    Lagrange = Lagrange + y_nodes(j)*L_j;
end %[output:group:6b1b2e16]

%%
%[text] ## Step 4: Visualize the Result
%[text] The dashed lines show the individual Lagrange basis polynomials, while the thick dashed line represents the final interpolating polynomial that passes through all data points.

fplot(Lagrange,Interval,LineStyle="--",LineWidth=2) %[output:809e60b4]
hold off %[output:809e60b4]

